---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Leaderboard">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/tabulator-tables@6.3.0/dist/css/tabulator_simple.min.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <h1>Leaderboard</h1>
  <p>
    The goal is simple compounding: repeated <strong>round trips at your chosen
    threshold</strong> (adjustable below) on stocks that oscillate reliably while
    net-trending up. The ranking key is
    <strong>net legs/yr</strong> (the # column) — (up legs − down legs) per year of data —
    because a good oscillator needs both a favorable up/down ratio (quality) and a
    high leg count (frequency); neither alone is enough. Every row also shows its
    <strong>current price</strong> against its own best-fit <strong>trend
    price</strong> (the same least-squares growth line the chart explorer draws) so
    you can judge whether it's running above or below its own trend, rather than a
    computed "signal" telling you what to do. Five gates keep the ranking honest without a
    weighted score: downtrenders (📉, negative drift) rank last; parabolic runners
    (🚀, spiked 3x+ from a trailing 12-month low within the last 2 years — one-way regime
    shift, not a repeatable cycle) rank below steady names; dip-chainers (⛓️, a run of
    5+ consecutive down legs, or two-plus runs of 4+ — BUY signals that ride deep
    underwater) rank between the two; thin histories (🌱, fewer than 10 completed legs)
    and short histories (🐣, less price history than the lookback window — a newly-listed
    leveraged ETF can print plenty of legs on volatility alone without living through
    enough calendar time for the rate to mean anything) both rank just below the proven
    names. Consecutive down-legs are never treated as
    failures on their own — a stock that dipped three times before recovering is
    oscillation richness, until it crosses one of those gates. Investigate any ticker in
    the chart explorer.
  </p>

  <div class="sma-controls">
    <div>
      <label for="lb-threshold">
        Threshold <span id="lb-threshold-label" class="value-pill">10%</span>
      </label>
      <input id="lb-threshold" type="range" min="5" max="20" step="5" value="10" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <p id="bullish-meta"><em>Loading screen…</em></p>

  <div id="bullish-scatter" style="min-height:520px;"></div>

  <h2>Leaderboard</h2>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
    Don't see a stock?
    <a href="https://github.com/sivadotblog/everest/issues/new?template=add-ticker.md&title=Add+ticker%3A+XXXX&labels=add-ticker"
       target="_blank" rel="noopener"
       style="font-weight:600;">Open a GitHub issue to request it →</a>
  </p>

  <div id="tb-filters" class="tb-filter-bar">
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-ticker">Ticker / Name</label>
      <input type="text" id="f-ticker" placeholder="e.g. RKLB or Rocket Lab" autocomplete="off" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-rank-max">Rank ≤</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-rank-max" placeholder="e.g. 100" min="1" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-vstrend-max">vs trend ≤ %</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-vstrend-max" placeholder="e.g. 0" step="0.5" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label>CAGR%</label>
      <div class="tb-filter-range">
        <input type="number" id="f-cagr-min" placeholder="min" />
        <span>–</span>
        <input type="number" id="f-cagr-max" placeholder="max" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group tb-filter-group--btn">
      <button id="f-reset">Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="bullish-table"></div>

  <div class="field-legend">
    <h2>Field Descriptions</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>#</strong></td>
          <td>The ranking key: (up legs − down legs) per year of data. Rewards both a
          favorable up/down ratio and a high leg count — a ticker that rarely dips but
          barely moves (e.g. a slow, "safe" oscillator) scores low here just as a ticker
          that chops constantly near 1:1 does; the surplus is what compounds.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Name</strong></td>
          <td>Company / fund name, fetched from Yahoo Finance and cached alongside prices.
          Falls back to the ticker symbol if Yahoo has nothing for it. Search by name or
          ticker using the filter box above.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Price</strong></td>
          <td>Current price — the last available close.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Trend growth</strong></td>
          <td>Best-fit steady yearly growth over the lookback (least-squares line on log
          price — the chart's dashed line); unlike CAGR% it isn't hostage to the first
          and last day.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Trend price today</strong></td>
          <td>Where that line sits on the latest date — compare with <strong>Price</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>vs trend</strong></td>
          <td>Negative (orange) = below trend; sort ascending for names furthest below
          their own trend.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>n▲ / n▼</strong></td>
          <td>Raw counts of +N% and −N% legs (at the selected threshold) over the window —
          the evidence behind the rank, never hidden. A young listing can show a
          strong rate on few legs;
          check n▲ (also see 🌱 thin-history and 🐣 short-history flags).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>CAGR%</strong></td>
          <td>Growth context: annualized return over the lookback. Tickers with negative
          drift (📉, greyed) are not candidates no matter how much they oscillate — they
          rank below every trend-positive name.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MaxDD%</strong></td>
          <td>Volatility context: worst peak-to-trough drawdown over the lookback. The
          price of admission for the oscillations.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1y run↑%</strong></td>
          <td>The parabolic gate: the largest gain from the lowest close in any trailing
          12-month window over the lookback — daily closes vs. the trailing low, no
          averaging. QQQ/SPY sit around +40%; CIFR's $3 → $25 spike is +700%. At
          <strong>+200% (3x) or more within the last 2 years</strong> the ticker is flagged
          🚀, greyed, and ranked below every steady trend-positive name: those legs
          came from a one-way regime shift, not a repeatable dip-cycle. A spike older than
          2 years stays on the record but no longer demotes — a ticker that oscillated
          cleanly since is judged on its recent behavior, not stale evidence. Thresholds
          are configurable (<code>analyzer.parabolic_max_run_up_pct</code>,
          <code>analyzer.parabolic_recency_days</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>⛓️ Chained dips</strong></td>
          <td>A ticker whose down legs chain deep makes each BUY a weak promise — capital
          rides underwater before the harvest. Flagged (greyed, ranked between steady and
          parabolic names) when the longest run of consecutive down legs reaches 5
          (~−41% compounded from the first leg), or when runs of 4+ have happened twice or
          more (depth once is an episode; repeatedly is a pattern). Hover the ⛓️ badge on a
          ticker for its worst-run depth and count.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>🌱 Thin history</strong></td>
          <td>Fewer than 10 completed legs — not enough evidence for the rank to
          mean anything (a new leveraged ETF can print a flattering rate on a handful of
          legs). Flagged and ranked just below the proven steady names.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>🐣 Short history</strong></td>
          <td>The ticker's actual price history is shorter than the lookback window —
          different problem than 🌱, same root cause. A newly-listed, highly volatile
          single-stock leveraged ETF can clear the thin-history bar (10+ legs) purely on
          leverage-inflated chop packed into a single year, then rank near the top of the
          board on a rate computed over a span it never lived through. Flagged when actual
          history is under <code>lookback_years × analyzer.min_history_fraction</code>
          (default 0.9) and ranked at the same tier as 🌱.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Recent Δ</strong></td>
          <td>Every threshold crossing at the selected ±N% in the last 30 days, oldest to newest — e.g. <code>-1↓ -1↓ +1↑</code> for two down-legs then an up-leg. Based on the actual sequence of events, not calendar days, so it isn't affected by weekends/holidays and naturally shows more badges for an active stock and fewer for a quiet one.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="https://unpkg.com/tabulator-tables@6.3.0/dist/js/tabulator.min.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src={`${base}/js/bullish-screen.js`}></script>
</Base>
